# Variation introductions (IDEA claims: no move or square names allowed by the checker).
I = {}
def v(name, *sentences): I[name] = list(sentences)

# ---- Berlin ----
v("Berlin Defense", "Black develops the king's knight and attacks White's centre pawn at once instead of first questioning the bishop. It is one of the most solid defences at the top level and leads to very different games depending on how White protects the centre.")
v("Berlin Defense, Rio Gambit Accepted", "Black simply takes the centre pawn after White castles. White regains it by force, and the play splits into the queenless Berlin endgame and the rook-lift lines where White pressures Black's uncastled king along the open file.")
v("Berlin Defense, Anti-Berlin Variation", "White protects the centre pawn with a pawn and avoids the forcing endgame lines. The game stays complex with all pieces on the board; White aims for a slow build-up and a later central break, Black for active bishops and a solid centre.")
v("Berlin Defense, l'Hermet Variation", "Black's knight retreats instead of taking more material, inviting White to exchange on the queenside and open the centre. Most games lead to the famous queenless endgame where structure matters more than attack.")
v("Berlin Defense, l'Hermet Variation, Berlin Wall Defense", "The Berlin endgame: queens are off and Black's king has lost the right to castle, but Black has the bishop pair and a very solid position. White plays for the healthy kingside pawn majority; Black for activity of the bishops and patient defence.")
v("Berlin Defense, Berlin Wall", "A typical setup in the Berlin endgame where Black develops the light-squared bishop and brings the king towards the queenside. White tries to use space and the extra kingside pawn; Black aims to neutralise and later use the bishops.")
v("Open Berlin Defense, l'Hermet Variation", "White advances in the centre with tempo instead of exchanging on the queenside. Black's knights have to find new squares and Black usually returns material to complete development.")
v("Open Berlin Defense, Showalter Variation", "White keeps the light-squared bishop instead of exchanging it, accepting a slower development to keep more tension.")
v("Berlin Defense, Anti-Berlin Variation, Mortimer Variation", "Black re-routes the knight towards the kingside so that the c-pawn can support a later central advance. It is solid but slow, and White simply develops.")
v("Berlin Defense, Beverwijk Variation", "Black develops the dark-squared bishop actively and leaves the centre pawn to its fate for the moment. White can grab space in the centre with tempo.")
v("Berlin Defense, Improved Steinitz Defense", "Black protects the centre pawn with a pawn; solid but passive, it gives White a free hand to build the ideal centre.")

# ---- Exchange ----
v("Exchange Variation", "White gives up the Spanish bishop at once to change the pawn structure. Black gets the bishop pair and fast development; White gets a healthy kingside pawn majority that can become decisive in an endgame.")
v("Exchange Variation, Normal Variation", "White castles and keeps the option of opening the centre. Black usually protects the centre pawn and develops quickly to make the bishop pair count before the endgame.")
v("Exchange Variation, Alapin Gambit", "Black offers material to open lines towards White's king, using the bishop pair and active pieces. White must decide whether taking is worth the open files.")
v("Exchange Variation, Keres Variation", "White develops the queen's knight first to keep options open and support the centre before castling.")
v("Exchange Variation, Gligoric Variation", "Black protects the centre pawn with the f-pawn, a solid setup that keeps the bishop pair but slightly weakens the light squares near Black's king.")
v("Exchange Variation, King's Bishop Variation", "Black defends the centre pawn with the dark-squared bishop, developing quickly at the cost of a slightly passive bishop.")
v("Exchange Variation, Bronstein Variation", "Black protects the centre pawn with the queen and prepares to castle queenside, aiming for active piece play.")

# ---- Open ----
v("Open", "Black takes White's centre pawn and keeps it for the moment, accepting an open, dynamic position. Black gets free piece play; White targets the slightly loose black centre and the uncastled king.")
v("Open, Main Line", "Both sides follow the classical setup: Black supports the knight in the centre and develops the light-squared bishop, White gains space with a central pawn and prepares to undermine Black's pieces.")
v("Open, Bernstein Variation", "White challenges Black's centre knight with the queen's knight at once, aiming to exchange Black's most active piece.")
v("Open, Classical Defense", "Black develops the dark-squared bishop to a modest square, choosing safety and a solid structure over maximum activity.")
v("Open, Italian Variation", "Black puts the dark-squared bishop on its most active diagonal, aimed at White's kingside, accepting sharper play.")
v("Open, Howell Attack", "White develops the queen to pressure Black's queenside pawns and prepare a rook on the d-file.")
v("Open, St. Petersburg Variation", "White challenges Black's centre knight while Black's bishop is active, leading to sharp tactical play around White's kingside.")
v("Open, Dilworth Variation", "Black sacrifices a knight for two pawns and a powerful attack along the open file towards White's king. It is double-edged and very concrete.")

# ---- Closed ----
v("Closed", "Black develops calmly and prepares to castle, choosing the classical closed Spanish. Both sides manoeuvre for a long time before the central break; plans matter more than early tactics.")
v("Closed, Martinez Variation", "White protects the centre pawn with a pawn and delays the classical central setup. It is a modern, flexible system that keeps the game slow and avoids the Marshall gambit.")
v("Closed, Delayed Exchange", "White exchanges the bishop for the knight one move later than in the Exchange Variation, so Black has already committed the bishop to a modest square.")
v("Closed, Breyer Defense", "Black's queen's knight retreats to its starting square to reroute and support the centre, freeing the c-pawn and the light-squared bishop. One of the most respected and solid main lines.")
v("Closed, Breyer", "White strikes in the centre immediately while Black's knight is regrouping.")
v("Closed, Breyer Defense, Zaitsev Hybrid", "Black completes the Breyer knight regrouping. White's typical plan is to bring the queen's knight to the kingside and later decide when to close or open the centre.")
v("Closed, Flohr System", "Black develops the light-squared bishop to the long diagonal to increase pressure on White's centre pawn, as in the Zaitsev.")
v("Closed, Chigorin Defense", "Black's knight attacks the Spanish bishop and goes to the edge to support queenside expansion. Black gains space on the queenside; White keeps the strong centre.")
v("Closed, Zaitsev System", "Black puts pressure on White's centre pawn with the rook and the long-diagonal bishop. Very rich and theoretical; White must watch for counterplay against the centre.")
v("Closed, Bogoljubow Variation", "White strikes in the centre before preventing Black's pin, and Black uses the pin on the knight to increase pressure on White's centre.")
v("Closed, Smyslov Defense", "Black prevents White's minor pieces from using a kingside square before regrouping, a solid prophylactic system.")
v("Closed, Yates Variation", "White breaks in the centre at once without the usual preparatory pawn move on the kingside, allowing Black's pin.")
v("Closed, Smyslov-Breyer-Zaitsev Hybrid", "Black combines the Zaitsev bishop setup with a prophylactic pawn move, a modern flexible way to hold the centre.")
v("Closed, Pilnik Variation", "White protects the centre pawn with a pawn first and sets up the centre slowly, avoiding sharp theory.")
v("Closed, Keres Defense", "In the Chigorin structure Black reroutes the king's knight to support the centre and prepare a kingside pawn advance.")
v("Closed, Kholmov Variation", "Black challenges the Spanish bishop with the light-squared bishop, offering a bishop trade to reduce White's pressure.")
v("Closed, Anti-Marshall", "White avoids the Marshall gambit by attacking Black's advanced queenside pawn first. Black must decide how to protect the queenside before the centre opens.")

# ---- Marshall ----
v("Marshall Attack", "Black sacrifices a centre pawn to open lines and attack White's kingside. White wins a pawn but must defend accurately; Black's compensation is long-lasting activity.")
v("Marshall Attack, Modern Variation", "Black protects the knight with a pawn and keeps the pieces aimed at White's king, the most popular way to continue the Marshall.")
v("Marshall Attack, Main Line", "White strengthens the centre and prepares to defend the kingside light squares while Black's queen and bishops gather for the attack.")
v("Marshall Attack, Modern Main Line", "Black's queen invades near White's king. White blocks the kingside and tries to complete development before Black's attack breaks through.")

# ---- Morphy other ----
v("Morphy Defense", "Black asks the bishop to decide at once. Most strong players retreat it to keep the Spanish pressure; the exchange gives the Exchange Variation.")
v("Morphy Defense, Anderssen Variation", "White protects the centre pawn with a pawn before castling, a calm modern setup that keeps many options and avoids early forcing play.")
v("Morphy Defense, Modern Steinitz Defense", "Black protects the centre pawn with a pawn before developing the king's knight, a solid setup where Black can choose between holding and releasing the centre.")
v("Morphy Defense, Neo-Arkhangelsk Variation", "Black develops the dark-squared bishop actively after gaining queenside space, aiming at White's kingside and the centre. Sharp, modern and very popular among strong players.")
v("Morphy Defense, Steinitz Deferred", "Black protects the centre pawn with a pawn after the knight is out, a solid but slightly passive system that often leads to a kingside fianchetto.")
v("Morphy Defense, Tarrasch Variation", "White develops the queen's knight early to protect the centre pawn, keeping the position simple and flexible.")
v("Morphy Defense, Cozio Defense", "Black develops the king's knight to a modest square to keep the f-pawn free and avoid pins. Unusual but playable.")
v("Morphy Defense, Møller Variation", "Black develops the dark-squared bishop to its most active diagonal before castling, inviting White to build a big centre with gain of time.")
v("Morphy Defense, Arkhangelsk Variation", "Black puts the light-squared bishop on the long diagonal to pressure White's centre pawn, often sacrificing a pawn for activity.")
v("Morphy Defense, Caro Variation", "Black gains queenside space early and drives the bishop back, at the cost of slightly loosening the queenside.")
v("Morphy Defense, Schliemann Defense Deferred", "Black strikes with the f-pawn after first asking the bishop, a sharp gambit-style approach.")
v("Morphy Defense, Classical Defense Deferred", "Black develops the dark-squared bishop actively before the king's knight, aiming at White's kingside and keeping the centre flexible.")

# ---- Schliemann / Classical / others ----
v("Schliemann Defense", "Black strikes at White's centre pawn with the f-pawn at once, a sharp gambit-style defence. White can keep it calm or enter forcing lines where accurate play is essential.")
v("Schliemann Defense, Dyckhoff Variation", "White develops the queen's knight and invites the sharpest lines, where the centre opens quickly.")
v("Schliemann Defense, Tartakower Variation", "Black develops the king's knight to contest the centre after the exchange, aiming for quick development.")
v("Classical Variation", "Black develops the dark-squared bishop to its most active diagonal at once. White usually builds a big centre with gain of time against the bishop.")
v("Classical Variation, Central Variation", "White prepares to build the classical pawn centre, gaining time against Black's active bishop.")
v("Cozio Defense", "Black develops the king's knight to a modest square to avoid pins and keep the f-pawn flexible.")
v("Fianchetto Defense", "Black prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop on the long diagonal, a hypermodern setup that lets White build a big centre.")
v("Bird Variation", "Black offers a knight trade at once. It costs time, and White usually gets a comfortable lead in development.")

