p(N+" c3", "White prepares a central pawn advance that will drive back Black's active bishop. Black supports the bishop with a pawn first.")
p(M+" d6 Re1 Bd7 c3", "White prepares a full pawn centre against Black's solid setup. Black often fianchettoes next.")
p(M+" Bc5 c3 b5 Bb3", "The bishop retreats to its best diagonal. Black now chooses between supporting the bishop with a pawn and a sharper central strike.")
p(E+" Ba4 Nf6 d3 d6 c3 g6 O-O", "White castles in the quiet structure; both sides prepare slow manoeuvres before any central break.")
p(N+" a4 Rb8", "Black protects the queenside pawn with the rook, keeping the active bishop. White continues with central or queenside pressure.")
p(N+" c3 d6 d4", "White advances in the centre, hitting the bishop. Black retreats it to a protected square on the diagonal and keeps pressure on the centre.")
p(M+" d6 Re1 Bd7 c3 g6 d4", "White builds the full pawn centre. Black's fianchetto bishop will pressure it while Black keeps the solid centre pawn.")
p(N+" a4 Rb8 c3", "White prepares a central advance while the queenside tension remains. Black supports the bishop with a pawn next.")
p(N+" c3 d6 a4", "White combines the queenside strike with the central preparation, asking Black's queenside pawns to commit.")
p(M+" d6 Re1 Bd7 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 h3", "White prevents any pin and keeps the full centre. A slow maneuvering battle follows, very similar to the classical closed structures.")
p(N+" c3 d6 d4 Bb6 Be3", "White develops the bishop to oppose Black's active bishop, aiming to exchange it and reduce Black's pressure on the centre.")
p(N+" a4 Rb8 c3 d6 d4 Bb6 a5", "White pushes the a-pawn to drive Black's bishop off its diagonal and gain queenside space.")
p(M+" d6 Re1 Bd7 c3 g6 d4 Bg7 h3 O-O Bc2", "White retreats the bishop to support the centre and protect the king's diagonal. A typical slow closed-style position.")
p(N+" a4 Rb8 c3 d6 d4 Bb6 a5 Ba7 h3", "White prevents the bishop pin and keeps all options. Black's bishop is awkwardly placed but still eyes White's kingside.")
p("e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 f5 d3", "White meets the Schliemann calmly, protecting the centre pawn and keeping a solid position. Black's f-pawn break has less punch, and White aims to exploit the loosened black kingside.")
p(E+" Ba4 Bc5 c3", "White prepares a central pawn advance that will hit Black's active bishop. Black usually develops the knight to its alternative square to keep the centre supported.")
p(E+" Ba4 Bc5 O-O", "White castles first and keeps the central advance in reserve. Black develops the king's knight towards the kingside.")
p(E+" Ba4 Bc5 O-O Nge7", "Black develops the knight to a square from which it supports the centre and avoids pins, a flexible idea against the castled king.")
p(E+" Ba4 Bc5 c3 Nge7 d4", "White builds the full centre with tempo on the bishop. Black must decide whether to take or keep the tension.")
p("e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5", "The Ruy Lopez: White develops the bishop to attack the knight that protects Black's centre pawn. Its pressure is slow but lasting, and it has been a main opening at every level for centuries.")
p("e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 Nge7 Nc3", "Against the Cozio setup White develops naturally and keeps central options. Black usually reroutes the knight to the kingside.")
p("e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 Nge7 Nc3 Ng6", "Black's knight goes to the kingside, supporting the centre pawn and freeing the bishop. White aims for a quick central break.")
